.TH ROBOCLIENT 8
.SH NAME
roboclient \- IPv4 DHCP client
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B roboclient
[\fB\-fqn\fR] [\fB\-m\fR \fImac\fR] [\fB\-h\fR \fIname\fR] [\fB\-s\fR \fIscript\fR] [\fB\-p\fR \fIpidfile\fR] [\fB\-t\fR \fItries\fR] \fIinterface\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B roboclient
obtains an IPv4 address, netmask, router, DNS servers and domain from a DHCP
server (dnsmasq, Kea, Microsoft DHCP, ISC dhcpd, ...) and keeps the lease
renewed.  It talks over an ordinary UDP socket on port 68 and asks the server
to reply by broadcast, so it needs no BPF or raw sockets.
.PP
Once the lease is bound the client runs
.I /usr/local/share/roboclient/roboclient.script
with the reason (PREINIT, BOUND, RENEW, EXPIRE or TIMEOUT) as its argument and
the lease in the environment, then goes to the background.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-f
Stay in the foreground.
.TP
.B \-q
Quit after the first lease is bound instead of renewing it.
.TP
.B \-n
Do not give the interface a 0.0.0.0 address first, even if it has none.
.TP
.BI \-m " mac"
Hardware address of the interface (for example 0:0:c0:12:34:56).  If not given,
it is read from MAC_\fIinterface\fR in
.I /usr/local/etc/roboclient.conf
or looked up in the ARP table.
.TP
.BI \-h " name"
Host name to announce.
.TP
.BI \-s " script"
Use another script.
.TP
.BI \-p " file"
Write the process id to the file.
.TP
.BI \-t " n"
Give up after n DISCOVER attempts (default 3, 0 means keep trying).
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.B SIGUSR1
Renew the lease now.
.TP
.B SIGTERM
Exit.  The interface is left configured.
.SH FILES
.nf
/usr/local/etc/roboclient.conf
/usr/local/etc/rc.dhcp   boot-time wrapper (call it from /etc/rc)
/usr/local/var/roboclient/\fIinterface\fR.info   the current lease
.fi
